    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBy May 1971, Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2016, bequeathed to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] According to a dated slide in the Griffin archive.\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"The phenomenon of synesthesia—in which one sensation (such as a color) accompanies the stimulation of another sense (such as sound)—explains the ﬂoral forms of these ear ornaments, originally worn by men and women alike. The ancient Maya believed that the eyes, nose, mouth, and ears were conduits of the vital essences of enlivened bodies. Breath, scent, voice, and hearing were all considered manifestations of ik’ (breath, wind, animating force). Floral motifs of this kind make smell visible, alluding to the pleasant fragrance of ik’, the distinctive essence of nobility and rulers, which signaled great refinement and beauty.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-2_CLA_FA_9_17_25.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
